         "type": "part",
         "label": "b",
         "prompt": "Find the limiting sum in terms of $x$.",
         "answerSpaceMm": 25,
         "answer": {
          "short": "$S_\\infty=\\dfrac{5}{1-2x}$",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}S_\\infty&=\\frac{a}{1-r}\\\\&=\\frac{5}{1-2x}\\end{align*}$$"
         },
         "questionDiagrams": []
        },
        {
         "id": "v2-9f332-p25-practice-j13-082840-j13-q-c",
         "type": "part",
         "label": "c",
         "prompt": "Find the set of values that the limiting sum can take.",
         "answerSpaceMm": 50,
         "answer": {
          "short": "$S_\\infty>\\frac52$",
          "worked": "For $-\\frac12<x<\\frac12$:\n$$\\begin{align*}-1&<-2x<1\\\\0&<1-2x<2\\end{align*}$$\nSo $\\dfrac{1}{1-2x}>\\dfrac12$, and $S_\\infty=\\dfrac{5}{1-2x}>\\dfrac52$.\n\nThe value $\\frac52$ would need $x=-\\frac12$, which is not allowed, so $S_\\infty$ never equals $\\frac52$. As $x\\to\\frac12$, $S_\\infty$ grows without bound."
         },
